 },
 "script": {
  "long": {
   "parts": [
    {
     "chapter": "",
     "sentences": [
      {
       "text": "Hey everyone, Vinícius here, and this is Hidden Cost.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "capa",
        "foto": "bend",
        "wiki": [
         "File:The Last Blockbuster storefront.jpg",
         "Blockbuster Bend Oregon"
        ],
        "seta": false,
        "recorte": "Netflix · 2000",
        "faixa": "Netflix vs Blockbuster",
        "linhas": [
         [
          "$50",
          "gold",
          260
         ],
         [
          "MILLION",
          "white",
          200
         ],
         [
          "REJECTED",
          "red",
          200
         ]
        ],
        "selo": "Netflix vs Blockbuster"
       },
       "abertura": true
      },
      {
       "text": "The year is 2000. Two guys from a tiny company walk into the office of the CEO of the biggest video rental chain in the world.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "date",
        "month": "TEXAS",
        "day": "USA",
        "year": "2000",
        "tag": "BLOCKBUSTER HQ"
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "Their offer: sell their company for fifty million dollars.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "counter",
        "value": 50,
        "prefix": "$",
        "suffix": "M",
        "over": "THE OFFER",
        "label": "FOR THE WHOLE COMPANY",
        "color": "gold",
        "size": 230
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "According to one of the founders, the Blockbuster CEO could barely keep a straight face.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bigtext",
        "stagger": 0.25,
        "lines": [
         [
          "HE ALMOST",
          "white",
          140
         ],
         [
          "LAUGHED",
          "red",
          200
         ]
        ]
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "That tiny company was called Netflix.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "flash",
        "text": "NETFLIX",
        "tint": "red",
        "size": 190
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "A company that mailed DVDs and was losing money.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bigtext",
        "stagger": 0.25,
        "lines": [
         [
          "DVDS BY MAIL",
          "white",
          130
         ],
         [
          "AND LOSSES",
          "red",
          160
         ]
        ]
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "Ten years later, Blockbuster filed for bankruptcy. And Netflix became one of the biggest entertainment companies on the planet.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "compare",
        "title": "TEN YEARS LATER",
        "items": [
         [
          "BANKRUPT",
          "BLOCKBUSTER · 2010",
          "red"
         ],
         [
          "300M+",
          "NETFLIX SUBSCRIBERS",
          "gold"
         ]
        ],
        "size": 150,
        "stagger": 1.2,
        "vs": false
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "But the story everyone tells, that Blockbuster died because it laughed at Netflix, is wrong.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "stamp",
        "org": "THE FAMOUS VERSION",
        "title": "IT DIED BECAUSE IT LAUGHED",
        "stamp": "WRONG",
        "stamp_at": 0.9
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "The real reason is a lot more interesting.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bigtext",
        "stagger": 0.25,
        "lines": [
         [
          "THE REAL",
          "white",
          150
         ],
         [
          "REASON",
          "gold",
          190
         ]
        ]
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "In this video: the rental empire, the fifty million dollar meeting, the war Netflix almost lost, and the mistake that actually killed Blockbuster.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bullets",
        "items": [
         "THE RENTAL EMPIRE",
         "THE $50M MEETING",
         "THE WAR",
         "THE REAL MISTAKE"
        ],
        "size": 80,
        "last_gold": true
       }
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "chapter": "THE RENTAL EMPIRE",
     "sentences": [
      {
       "text": "Blockbuster opened its first store in 1985, in Dallas, Texas.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "image",
        "media": "bb_loja",
        "wiki": [
         "File:Anchorage Blockbuster video store (41894010352).jpg"
        ],
        "lower": "Blockbuster",
        "dim": 0.4,
        "fallback": {
         "type": "bigtext",
         "stagger": 0.25,
         "lines": [
          [
           "1985",
           "gold",
           230
          ],
          [
           "THE FIRST STORE",
           "white",
           110
          ]
         ]
        }
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "In 1987, businessman Wayne Huizenga took control and put expansion on steroids.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bigtext",
        "stagger": 0.25,
        "lines": [
         [
          "1987",
          "gold",
          220
         ],
         [
          "WAYNE HUIZENGA",
          "white",
          120
         ],
         [
          "TAKES OVER",
          "muted",
          90
         ]
        ]
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "In 1994, he sold the chain to Viacom for seven point six billion dollars.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "counter",
        "value": 7,
        "prefix": "$",
        "suffix": ".6B",
        "over": "VIACOM BUYS IT · 1994",
        "label": "BLOCKBUSTER'S PRICE TAG",
        "color": "gold",
        "size": 220
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "Within a few years, it was the biggest video rental chain in the world.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "image",
        "media": "bb_prateleira",
        "wiki": [
         "File:Anchorage Blockbuster video store (41894012562).jpg"
        ],
        "lower": "The world's biggest rental chain",
        "dim": 0.4,
        "fallback": {
         "type": "bigtext",
         "stagger": 0.25,
         "lines": [
          [
           "THE BIGGEST",
           "white",
           150
          ],
          [
           "IN THE WORLD",
           "gold",
           150
          ]
         ]
        }
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "At its peak, in 2004, it had more than nine thousand stores worldwide.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "counter",
        "value": 9,
        "prefix": "",
        "suffix": ",000+ STORES",
        "over": "PEAK · 2004",
        "label": "BLOCKBUSTER WORLDWIDE",
        "color": "gold",
        "size": 180
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "Friday night, the whole family drove to Blockbuster to pick the weekend movie. It was almost a ritual.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "video",
        "media": "familia_sofa",
        "search": "family watching movie popcorn couch",
        "search_en": "family watching movie popcorn couch",
        "start": 0.0,
        "dim": 0.4,
        "lower": "Friday night",
        "fallback": {
         "type": "bigtext",
         "stagger": 0.25,
         "lines": [
          [
           "FRIDAY",
           "white",
           160
          ],
          [
           "NIGHT",
           "gold",
           170
          ]
         ]
        },
        "pixabay_id": 190117
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "The blue and yellow sign was everywhere, from big cities to small towns.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "image",
        "media": "bb_azul",
        "wiki": [
         "File:Blockbuster Video store Rockhampton.jpg"
        ],
        "lower": "Blue and yellow",
        "dim": 0.4,
        "fallback": {
         "type": "bigtext",
         "stagger": 0.25,
         "lines": [
          [
           "BLUE",
           "white",
           160
          ],
          [
           "AND YELLOW",
           "gold",
           160
          ]
         ]
        }
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "And there was one detail almost everyone remembers: late fees.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "stamp",
        "org": "LATE RETURN",
        "title": "TAPE RETURNED 2 DAYS LATE",
        "stamp": "LATE FEE",
        "stamp_at": 0.9
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "Forgot to return the tape? You paid. And Blockbuster made a lot of money from that.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bigtext",
        "stagger": 0.25,
        "lines": [
         [
          "LATE FEES",
          "white",
          150
         ],
         [
          "WERE PROFIT",
          "gold",
          170
         ]
        ]
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "In 2000 alone, late fees brought in around eight hundred million dollars. Sixteen percent of the company's entire revenue.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "print",
        "media": "print_multas",
        "url": "https://www.nbcnews.com/id/wbna39332696",
        "source": "NBC News · 2010",
        "rotate": -1.0,
        "fallback": {
         "type": "counter",
         "value": 800,
         "prefix": "$",
         "suffix": "M",
         "over": "LATE FEES ALONE · 2000",
         "label": "16% OF REVENUE",
         "color": "gold",
         "size": 210
        }
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "Remember that, because it matters at the end of the story.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "flash",
        "text": "REMEMBER\nTHIS",
        "color": "gold",
        "size": 160
       }
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "chapter": "DVDS BY MAIL",
     "sentences": [
      {
       "text": "In 1997, Reed Hastings and Marc Randolph founded Netflix in California.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "image",
        "media": "hastings",
        "wiki": [
         "File:Reed Hastings 2010 A.jpg"
        ],
        "lower": "Reed Hastings, Netflix co-founder",
        "dim": 0.4,
        "fallback": {
         "type": "bigtext",
         "stagger": 0.25,
         "lines": [
          [
           "1997",
           "gold",
           230
          ],
          [
           "REED HASTINGS",
           "white",
           110
          ],
          [
           "MARC RANDOLPH",
           "white",
           110
          ]
         ]
        }
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "The idea was to rent DVDs online and ship them by mail.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "video",
        "media": "correio",
        "search": "mailbox envelope mail delivery",
        "search_en": "mailbox envelope mail delivery",
        "start": 0.0,
        "dim": 0.4,
        "lower": "DVDs by mail",
        "fallback": {
         "type": "bigtext",
         "stagger": 0.25,
         "lines": [
          [
           "DVDS",
           "gold",
           220
          ],
          [
           "BY MAIL",
           "white",
           140
          ]
         ]
        },
        "pixabay_id": 43658
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "The website went live in 1998, with fewer than a thousand titles.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bigtext",
        "stagger": 0.25,
        "lines": [
         [
          "1998",
          "gold",
          220
         ],
         [
          "THE SITE GOES LIVE",
          "white",
          110
         ]
        ]
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "And there's a famous legend about how it all started.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "flash",
        "text": "THE LEGEND",
        "color": "gold",
        "size": 170
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "Reed Hastings said he got the idea after paying a forty dollar late fee for Apollo 13.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "print",
        "media": "print_apollo",
        "url": "https://www.cnbc.com/2017/05/23/netflix-ceo-reed-hastings-on-how-the-company-was-born.html",
        "source": "CNBC · 2017",
        "rotate": -1.0,
        "fallback": {
         "type": "stamp",
         "org": "LATE FEE",
         "title": "APOLLO 13",
         "stamp": "$40",
         "stamp_at": 0.9
        }
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "But the other co-founder, Marc Randolph, calls that story a convenient fiction. A good way to explain why Netflix was better.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "image",
        "media": "randolph",
        "wiki": [
         "File:Marc Randolph by Gage Skidmore.jpg"
        ],
        "lower": "Marc Randolph, Netflix co-founder",
        "dim": 0.4,
        "fallback": {
         "type": "bigtext",
         "stagger": 0.25,
         "lines": [
          [
           "CONVENIENT",
           "white",
           150
          ],
          [
           "FICTION?",
           "red",
           170
          ]
         ]
        }
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "True or not, late fees were exactly the weak spot Netflix wanted to attack.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bigtext",
        "stagger": 0.25,
        "lines": [
         [
          "THE WEAK SPOT",
          "white",
          130
         ],
         [
          "LATE FEES",
          "red",
          180
         ]
        ]
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "Then came the subscription: one flat monthly price, and no late fees.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "compare",
        "title": "THE MODEL",
        "items": [
         [
          "LATE FEES",
          "BLOCKBUSTER",
          "red"
         ],
         [
          "NO LATE FEES",
          "NETFLIX",
          "gold"
         ]
        ],
        "size": 150,
        "stagger": 1.2,
        "vs": true
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "Great for customers. For the company, it was a money pit.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bigtext",
        "stagger": 0.25,
        "lines": [
         [
          "GREAT FOR YOU",
          "white",
          130
         ],
         [
          "A MONEY PIT",
          "red",
          160
         ]
        ]
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "Around 2000, Netflix was losing about fifty million dollars a year and was drowning in debt.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "counter",
        "value": 50,
        "prefix": "-$",
        "suffix": "M",
        "over": "LOSSES PER YEAR",
        "label": "NETFLIX · 2000",
        "color": "red",
        "size": 210
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "That's the situation when the founders got on a plane to Texas.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "video",
        "media": "aviao",
        "search": "airplane takeoff",
        "search_en": "airplane takeoff",
        "start": 0.0,
        "dim": 0.4,
        "lower": "Headed to Texas",
        "fallback": {
         "type": "bigtext",
         "stagger": 0.25,
         "lines": [
          [
           "HEADED TO",
           "white",
           140
          ],
          [
           "TEXAS",
           "gold",
           200
          ]
         ]
        },
        "pixabay_id": 264862
       }
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "chapter": "THE FIFTY MILLION",
     "sentences": [
      {
       "text": "The pitch was simple: Blockbuster buys Netflix for fifty million dollars.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "counter",
        "value": 50,
        "prefix": "$",
        "suffix": "M",
        "over": "THE OFFER",
        "label": "NETFLIX FOR SALE",
        "color": "gold",
        "size": 230
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "Netflix would run the online side under the Blockbuster brand, and the stores would promote it.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "flow",
        "a": "NETFLIX",
        "a_sub": "runs online",
        "b": "BLOCKBUSTER",
        "b_sub": "brand and stores",
        "caption": "THE PROPOSED DEAL"
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "Netflix's CFO, Barry McCarthy, was in the room too.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bigtext",
        "stagger": 0.25,
        "lines": [
         [
          "IN THE ROOM",
          "white",
          140
         ],
         [
          "3 FROM NETFLIX",
          "gold",
          150
         ]
        ]
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "At the time, Blockbuster was bringing in around five billion dollars a year.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "compare",
        "title": "HOW BIG EACH ONE WAS",
        "items": [
         [
          "$5B",
          "BLOCKBUSTER REVENUE",
          "gold"
         ],
         [
          "$50M",
          "NETFLIX PRICE",
          "white"
         ]
        ],
        "size": 150,
        "stagger": 1.2,
        "vs": false
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "Fifty million was about one percent of that.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bigtext",
        "stagger": 0.25,
        "lines": [
         [
          "ABOUT",
          "white",
          130
         ],
         [
          "1%",
          "gold",
          260
         ]
        ]
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "CEO John Antioco said no. And according to Marc Randolph, he looked like he was trying not to laugh.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "print",
        "media": "print_fortune",
        "url": "https://fortune.com/2023/04/14/netflix-cofounder-marc-randolph-recalls-blockbuster-rejecting-chance-to-buy-it/",
        "source": "Fortune · 14/04/2023",
        "rotate": -1.0,
        "fallback": {
         "type": "stamp",
         "org": "BLOCKBUSTER · 2000",
         "title": "BUY NETFLIX",
         "stamp": "REJECTED",
         "stamp_at": 0.9
        }
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "According to reports, Netflix tried again a few more times. The answer was always no.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bigtext",
        "stagger": 0.25,
        "lines": [
         [
          "MORE TRIES",
          "white",
          140
         ],
         [
          "ALWAYS NO",
          "red",
          170
         ]
        ]
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "And one detail shows exactly where Blockbuster was placing its bets.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "flash",
        "text": "ONE DETAIL",
        "color": "gold",
        "size": 160
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "That same year, it signed a twenty year deal with Enron for movies on demand over the internet.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "stamp",
        "org": "BLOCKBUSTER + ENRON · 2000",
        "title": "20-YEAR DEAL",
        "stamp": "SIGNED",
        "stamp_at": 0.9
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "Months later, Enron collapsed in one of the biggest accounting scandals in history. And the deal went down with it.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bigtext",
        "stagger": 0.25,
        "lines": [
         [
          "ENRON",
          "white",
          170
         ],
         [
          "COLLAPSED",
          "red",
          180
         ]
        ]
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "But here's my take: saying no that day wasn't stupid.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "flash",
        "text": "MY\nTAKE",
        "color": "gold",
        "size": 170
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "Netflix was losing money, depended on the mail, and streaming didn't even exist yet.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bullets",
        "title": "NETFLIX IN 2000",
        "items": [
         "LOSING MONEY",
         "DEPENDED ON THE MAIL",
         "NO STREAMING"
        ],
        "size": 90,
        "last_gold": true
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "Blockbuster's mistake wasn't that no. It was what came next.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bigtext",
        "stagger": 0.25,
        "lines": [
         [
          "THE MISTAKE",
          "white",
          150
         ],
         [
          "CAME LATER",
          "red",
          160
         ]
        ]
       }
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "chapter": "THE WAR NETFLIX ALMOST LOST",
     "sentences": [
      {
       "text": "In 2004, Blockbuster launched its own online rental service.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "date",
        "month": "BLOCKBUSTER",
        "day": "ONLINE",
        "year": "2004",
        "tag": "RENTALS OVER THE INTERNET"
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "In about two years, it reached roughly two million subscribers.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "counter",
        "value": 2,
        "suffix": " MILLION",
        "over": "BLOCKBUSTER ONLINE",
        "label": "IN ABOUT 2 YEARS",
        "color": "gold",
        "size": 210
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "For comparison, Netflix had taken nine years to reach about six million.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "compare",
        "title": "SUBSCRIBERS",
        "items": [
         [
          "2M",
          "BLOCKBUSTER IN ~2 YEARS",
          "gold"
         ],
         [
          "6M",
          "NETFLIX IN ~9 YEARS",
          "white"
         ]
        ],
        "size": 160,
        "stagger": 1.2,
        "vs": false
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "In other words: in two years, Blockbuster built a third of what took Netflix almost a decade.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bigtext",
        "stagger": 0.25,
        "lines": [
         [
          "IN 2 YEARS",
          "white",
          140
         ],
         [
          "ONE THIRD",
          "gold",
          180
         ],
         [
          "OF NETFLIX",
          "white",
          120
         ]
        ]
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "And in 2006 came the strongest move: rent online, return in store, and swap for another movie on the spot.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "flow",
        "a": "ORDER ONLINE",
        "a_sub": "delivered home",
        "b": "SWAP IN STORE",
        "b_sub": "on the spot",
        "caption": "TOTAL ACCESS · 2006"
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "That was something Netflix simply couldn't offer.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bigtext",
        "stagger": 0.25,
        "lines": [
         [
          "NETFLIX",
          "white",
          150
         ],
         [
          "HAD NO STORES",
          "red",
          150
         ]
        ]
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "Think about it: you got DVDs at home, and when you wanted something right now, you stopped by the store around the corner. Best of both worlds.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bigtext",
        "stagger": 0.25,
        "lines": [
         [
          "THE BEST",
          "white",
          150
         ],
         [
          "OF BOTH WORLDS",
          "gold",
          130
         ]
        ]
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "And Blockbuster had an advantage money can't buy quickly: thousands of stores in every town.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bigtext",
        "stagger": 0.25,
        "lines": [
         [
          "THOUSANDS",
          "gold",
          180
         ],
         [
          "OF STORES",
          "white",
          150
         ]
        ]
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "In one quarter of 2007, Netflix actually lost fifty five thousand subscribers.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "counter",
        "value": 55,
        "prefix": "-",
        "suffix": "K",
        "over": "NETFLIX · 2007",
        "label": "SUBSCRIBERS LOST IN ONE QUARTER",
        "color": "red",
        "size": 220
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "Years later, Reed Hastings himself admitted: if it weren't for the debt, Blockbuster could have crushed them.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bigtext",
        "stagger": 0.25,
        "lines": [
         [
          "COULD HAVE",
          "white",
          140
         ],
         [
          "CRUSHED NETFLIX",
          "red",
          130
         ]
        ]
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "And Antioco later said that if his strategy had continued, Blockbuster could have reached around ten million online subscribers.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "counter",
        "value": 10,
        "suffix": " MILLION",
        "over": "ACCORDING TO ANTIOCO",
        "label": "WHAT BLOCKBUSTER ONLINE COULD HAVE HAD",
        "color": "gold",
        "size": 200
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "At that moment, the one winning the war was Blockbuster.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "flash",
        "text": "BLOCKBUSTER\nWINNING",
        "tint": "red",
        "size": 140
       }
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "chapter": "THE REAL MISTAKE",
     "sentences": [
      {
       "text": "But that war was expensive. To compete, Blockbuster had killed late fees, one of its biggest sources of cash.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bigtext",
        "stagger": 0.25,
        "lines": [
         [
          "NO LATE FEES",
          "white",
          140
         ],
         [
          "NO CASH",
          "red",
          180
         ]
        ]
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "And a fight broke out inside. Investor Carl Icahn pushed hard, and Antioco lost his job.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "image",
        "media": "icahn",
        "wiki": [
         "File:Carl Icahn, 1980s.jpg"
        ],
        "lower": "Carl Icahn, investor (1980s)",
        "dim": 0.4,
        "fallback": {
         "type": "flow",
         "a": "CARL ICAHN",
         "a_sub": "investor",
         "b": "ANTIOCO",
         "b_sub": "out",
         "caption": "THE BOARDROOM FIGHT"
        }
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "The new CEO raised the price of the online service and ended the free in-store swaps.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "stamp",
        "org": "NEW MANAGEMENT · 2007",
        "title": "FREE IN-STORE SWAPS",
        "stamp": "CANCELED",
        "stamp_at": 0.9
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "And spent money on other bets, like buying Movielink, an online movie service that never took off.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bigtext",
        "stagger": 0.25,
        "lines": [
         [
          "NEW BETS",
          "white",
          150
         ],
         [
          "NO RESULTS",
          "red",
          160
         ]
        ]
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "Growth stopped overnight.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bigtext",
        "stagger": 0.25,
        "lines": [
         [
          "GROWTH",
          "white",
          160
         ],
         [
          "STOPPED",
          "red",
          200
         ]
        ]
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "And here's the irony: years later, Antioco himself invested in Netflix. But he sold early, before the big run-up.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bigtext",
        "stagger": 0.25,
        "lines": [
         [
          "ANTIOCO",
          "white",
          150
         ],
         [
          "BOUGHT NETFLIX",
          "gold",
          120
         ],
         [
          "AND SOLD EARLY",
          "red",
          110
         ]
        ]
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "Meanwhile, also in 2007, Netflix launched streaming.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "date",
        "month": "NETFLIX",
        "day": "▶",
        "year": "2007",
        "tag": "STREAMING BEGINS"
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "In September 2010, Blockbuster filed for bankruptcy, with close to a billion dollars in debt.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "counter",
        "value": 900,
        "prefix": "$",
        "suffix": "M+",
        "over": "SEPTEMBER 2010",
        "label": "DEBT AT BANKRUPTCY",
        "color": "red",
        "size": 200
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "And this is the hidden cost.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "flash",
        "text": "THE HIDDEN\nCOST",
        "tint": "red",
        "size": 150
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "At the time, there were still around twenty five thousand employees. Thousands of stores closed, and a lot of people lost their jobs.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "image",
        "media": "bb_fechando",
        "wiki": [
         "File:Blockbuster Video store Closing, Miami Beach Florida, November 2013.jpg"
        ],
        "lower": "Store closing · Miami Beach, 2013",
        "dim": 0.4,
        "fallback": {
         "type": "bigtext",
         "stagger": 0.25,
         "lines": [
          [
           "STORES",
           "white",
           160
          ],
          [
           "CLOSED",
           "red",
           180
          ]
         ]
        }
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "In 2011, what was left was sold at auction for about three hundred twenty million dollars.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "print",
        "media": "print_dish",
        "url": "https://techcrunch.com/2011/04/06/dish-wins-bankruptcy-auction-buys-blockbuster-assets-for-228m-in-cash/",
        "source": "TechCrunch · 06/04/2011",
        "rotate": -1.0,
        "fallback": {
         "type": "compare",
         "title": "BLOCKBUSTER",
         "items": [
          [
           "$5B",
           "YEARLY REVENUE",
           "white"
          ],
          [
           "$320M",
           "SOLD IN 2011",
           "red"
          ]
         ],
         "size": 150,
         "stagger": 1.2,
         "vs": false
        }
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "If you remember renting movies at Blockbuster, subscribe to Hidden Cost. Every week, a new brand story.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bigtext",
        "stagger": 0.25,
        "lines": [
         [
          "SUBSCRIBE",
          "gold",
          160
         ],
         [
          "A NEW BRAND EVERY WEEK",
          "white",
          80
         ]
        ]
       }
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "chapter": "WHAT HAPPENED NEXT",
     "sentences": [
      {
       "text": "Today, there's only one Blockbuster left in the world, in Bend, Oregon. It became a tourist attraction.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "image",
        "media": "bend",
        "wiki": [
         "File:The Last Blockbuster storefront.jpg",
         "Blockbuster Bend Oregon"
        ],
        "lower": "The last Blockbuster · Bend, Oregon",
        "dim": 0.4,
        "fallback": {
         "type": "bigtext",
         "stagger": 0.25,
         "lines": [
          [
           "ONLY ONE",
           "white",
           150
          ],
          [
           "LEFT",
           "gold",
           210
          ]
         ]
        }
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "Netflix, on the other hand, went global, launching in Canada in 2010 and in Latin America in 2011.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "image",
        "media": "netflix_sede",
        "wiki": [
         "File:Netflix headquarters.jpg"
        ],
        "lower": "Netflix HQ · Los Gatos, California",
        "dim": 0.4,
        "fallback": {
         "type": "bigtext",
         "stagger": 0.25,
         "lines": [
          [
           "2010",
           "gold",
           220
          ],
          [
           "NETFLIX GOES GLOBAL",
           "white",
           100
          ]
         ]
        }
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "In 2013, it released House of Cards, its first big hit original series. And it stopped being a rental company to become a studio.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bigtext",
        "stagger": 0.25,
        "lines": [
         [
          "FROM RENTALS",
          "white",
          140
         ],
         [
          "TO A STUDIO",
          "gold",
          160
         ]
        ]
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "Today, Netflix has more than three hundred million subscribers.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "counter",
        "value": 300,
        "prefix": "",
        "suffix": "M+",
        "over": "NETFLIX",
        "label": "SUBSCRIBERS",
        "color": "gold",
        "size": 220
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "The hidden lesson is simple.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "flash",
        "text": "THE HIDDEN\nLESSON",
        "tint": "red",
        "size": 150
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "Every big company has its own late fee: easy money that customers hate.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bigtext",
        "stagger": 0.25,
        "lines": [
         [
          "EVERY COMPANY",
          "white",
          130
         ],
         [
          "HAS ITS LATE FEE",
          "red",
          130
         ]
        ]
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "As long as that money keeps coming in, nobody inside wants to touch it. And that's exactly when an outsider shows up and does it differently.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bigtext",
        "stagger": 0.25,
        "lines": [
         [
          "AN OUTSIDER",
          "white",
          140
         ],
         [
          "DOES IT DIFFERENTLY",
          "gold",
          110
         ]
        ]
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "What killed Blockbuster wasn't saying no. It was defending late fees, the stores and the debt, when customers already wanted something else.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bullets",
        "title": "WHAT KILLED IT",
        "items": [
         "THE DEBT",
         "THE BOARDROOM FIGHT",
         "DEFENDING THE OLD MODEL"
        ],
        "size": 90,
        "last_gold": true
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "What about you? What was the last movie you rented at a video store? Tell me in the comments.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bigtext",
        "stagger": 0.25,
        "lines": [
         [
          "WHAT WAS",
          "white",
          140
         ],
         [
          "YOUR LAST RENTAL?",
          "gold",
          130
         ],
         [
          "COMMENT BELOW",
          "muted",
          90
         ]
        ]
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "And the question is: which company today is laughing at the next Netflix?",
       "scene": {
        "type": "endcard",
        "sub": "NETFLIX VS BLOCKBUSTER"
       }
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
